**Who to ask**
- **Gonzalo Rodriguez** (`<email redacted>`, GitHub `grzuy`) wrote most of `store_proxy/`. He has about 60 commits across `lib/rack/attack/` and was still committing in March 2026. He is the best person to ask about why the proxies are shaped the way they are.
- **Santiago Bartesaghi** (`<email redacted>`) also touched this path, most recently in December 2024. The tool lists him as a fallback owner of the repo, alongside `grzuy`.

**Who should review**
- Gonzalo and Santiago.
- Aaron Suggs (42 commits in `lib/rack/attack/`) and fatkodima (16, including four recent-era commits to `store_proxy/` in October 2019) know the history. Neither shows up in the tool's active-owner list, so I don't know if they're still around.

**Caveats**
- The tool's dry-run only produced a rule for `/lib/rack/attack/`. It names Mei Tanaka, Hye-jin Park and Santiago.
- Mei and Hye-jin have only 1 and 2 commits in the whole repo. None of those commits touch `store_proxy/` in the `git log` I ran for that path.
- Their weight probably comes from broad changes across `lib/`, so treat their ownership of this code as unverified. I'd skip them as reviewers unless you know they own the area.
- The tool was run with `--no-github`, so it didn't map emails to GitHub accounts. Gonzalo's commits use two author names (`Gonzalo Rodriguez` and `Gonzalo`). I haven't confirmed that all his weight was merged into one person.
- Ownership here is inferred from commit counts, not from who actually reviews code.
- I used `--dry-run`, so no files were written to the repo.

